PROTOCOL = "# V35 — Shared Solver and Local Ownership Isolate\n## Refined preregistration v2 — Codex-ready\n\n## Status\n\n**Design only. No V35 scientific outcomes have been observed.**\n\nThis document supersedes both the first `V35_design.md` draft and the intermediate `V35_design_refined.md` draft.\n\nThe refinement is based on the full V24–V34 trajectory, with particular weight on the completed V33 and V34 multi-wave results. It preserves the useful two-stage fork from the first draft but removes several confounds from Stage II.\n\nV35 is deliberately narrow:\n\n1. **Stage I:** determine whether the remaining failure of shared parametric memory is mainly an online-solver failure by replacing the greedy directional writer with exact cumulative recursive ridge / RLS.\n2. **Stage II, conditional:** if Stage I does not qualify, isolate whether **item-local ownership of state** prevents overwrite. Stage II does **not** yet test bounded capacity, approximate retrieval, learned aliases, eviction, or a new fly-inspired routing system.\n\nMaximum scientific workload:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{64\\text{ trajectories}}\n\\]\n\nwith 32 guaranteed Stage-I trajectories and at most 32 conditional Stage-II trajectories.\n\nThere is no automatic Stage III.\n\n---\n\n# 1. What prior rounds have already settled\n\nV35 must not reopen mechanisms that the preceding program has already tested adequately.\n\n## 1.1 Representation and task validity\n\nV27 showed that apparent continual-learning improvements are uninterpretable when:\n\n- the frozen model has no adaptation headroom; or\n- “old memory” probes measure generic transfer instead of exact learned dependence.\n\nV28 therefore established the requirement for exact arbitrary old bindings, no old replay, and genuine new-learning headroom.\n\nV33 later established that **byte identity must remain bound to temporal position**. The exact lag96 code substantially improved same-key alias geometry and cross-rendering revision, but representation repair alone did not preserve old facts.\n\nTherefore V35 keeps:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{exact lag96 for the shared-storage isolate}}\n\\]\n\nand does not reopen encoder search in Stage I.\n\n## 1.2 Shared coefficient overwrite is real\n\nV29 isolated within-bank coefficient overwrite as the dominant storage damage in the overlap condition.\n\nV30 showed that full historical coactivation geometry can reduce that damage:\n\n\\[\nP=(I+\\sum zz^\\top)^{-1},\n\\qquad\nq_P=\\frac{Pz}{z^\\top Pz}.\n\\]\n\nDiagonal activity histories did not reproduce the effect.\n\nV31 then transferred the full-covariance mechanism to a genuinely causal no-label byte stream.\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{historical coactivation geometry is a validated interference-reduction primitive}}\n\\]\n\nbut not yet a persistent-memory solution.\n\n## 1.3 Global familiarity / error gating stays retired\n\nThe autonomous V31 familiarity+error rule opened more often on:\n\n- novel first-time contexts;\n- one-off noise;\n\nthan on genuine revisions, and a simpler error-only control largely matched it.\n\nV35 therefore contains **no global revision detector** and makes no claim that surprise/error itself identifies a revision.\n\n## 1.4 MESU / diagonal uncertainty stays parked\n\nThe MESU experiment established genuine metaplastic dynamics but largely moved along the stability–plasticity frontier. Infinite horizon reproduced catastrophic remembering.\n\nV35 therefore does not add a MESU arm or per-coordinate uncertainty mechanism.\n\n## 1.5 V33 separated representation from memory\n\nOn the same paired V32 streams:\n\n- lag ordinary retained only about 5.5% of untouched A after D;\n- lag full covariance retained about 21.9%;\n- full minus ordinary was about +16.4 percentage points;\n- current D remained 100%;\n- covariance reduced accumulated old-score disturbance;\n- alias revision improved mainly because of the lag representation, not because of covariance.\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\text{representation collision and shared-write interference are independent problems}\n}\n\\]\n\nand alias/generalization must remain separate from strict persistence.\n\n## 1.6 V34 largely falsified the “bad geometry / cap” explanation\n\nV34 introduced:\n\n- fixed 768-unit expansion;\n- sparse activation;\n- one versus four local compartments;\n- local block covariance.\n\nThe strongest C4-P arm improved the diagnostics that had looked pathological:\n\n- effective rank increased strongly;\n- covariance cap engagement fell from near-universal to only a few percent;\n- requested directional amplification fell;\n- same-key contexts had measurably greater overlap than different-key contexts.\n\nYet untouched A still collapsed to about 3%.\n\nThe especially important temporal pattern was:\n\n\\[\n100\\%\\rightarrow76.6\\%\\rightarrow7.0\\%\\rightarrow3.1\\%.\n\\]\n\nTherefore sparse expansion and compartment wiring can produce impressive **one-wave delay** without durable persistence.\n\nV35 consequently treats the following as demoted hypotheses:\n\n\\[\n\\cancel{\\text{low effective rank is the main cause}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\cancel{\\text{direction-cap saturation is the main cause}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\cancel{\\text{static sparse compartment wiring alone is sufficient}}\n\\]\n\n## 1.7 The exact dictionary remains a structural clue, not the answer\n\nAcross V32–V34, exact-context dictionary storage retained untouched exact contexts essentially perfectly while shared neural coefficients collapsed.\n\nIts cumulative-count update revised poorly because historical counts dominated the later changed labels.\n\nThat establishes neither that dictionaries are a final architecture nor that explicit memory cannot revise.\n\nThe useful distinction is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\text{shared coefficients are repeatedly rewritten;}\n\\quad\n\\text{item-local state has ownership.}\n}\n\\]\n\nV35 is designed to determine whether this distinction is actually causal after the remaining shared-solver confound is removed.\n\n---\n\n# 2. Fly-data interpretation retained, with limits\n\nThe BANC-derived structural audit motivates architecture, not a claim about the biological learning rule.\n\nThe useful observations are:\n\n- the canonical mushroom-body seed circuit is not structurally closed;\n- substantial multi-hop `MBON -> DAN` reachability uses surrounding superior-brain circuitry;\n- the olfactory side contains recurrent preprocessing;\n- readout coordination is structurally broader than recurrent DAN-to-DAN interaction;\n- static connectivity does not reveal synaptic eligibility, consolidation, metaplasticity, or the exact persistent state.\n\nThe minimal V35 abstraction is therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\text{shared statistical prediction}\n+\n\\text{local state ownership}\n+\n\\text{local teaching feedback}\n}\n\\]\n\nrather than another rearrangement of KC width or compartment count.\n\nV35 does **not** claim that:\n\n- the fly has an episodic byte dictionary;\n- MBON recurrence literally computes the equations below;\n- lateral horn is an episodic store;\n- DANs implement the exact residual rule used here.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Non-negotiable causal interface\n\nEvery live learner receives only:\n\n\\[\n\\text{past bytes}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{prediction}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{observed next byte}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{online update}.\n\\]\n\nThe learner receives no:\n\n- task ID;\n- wave ID;\n- record-position flag;\n- logical-key ID;\n- alias ID;\n- memory ID;\n- revision flag;\n- corruption flag;\n- future byte;\n- evaluator label.\n\nEvaluator annotations may be used only for probes, statistics, and explicitly labeled noncausal oracles.\n\nThere is:\n\n- no replay of untouched A;\n- no wave-boundary reset;\n- no model selection from V35 scientific seeds;\n- no post-result parameter tuning.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Fixed multi-wave workload\n\nReuse the exact V32/V33/V34 stream generator.\n\nEach full trajectory contains:\n\n\\[\nA\\rightarrow B\\rightarrow C\\rightarrow D.\n\\]\n\nPer trajectory:\n\n- 16 independent six-byte logical keys per wave;\n- 64 logical keys total;\n- arbitrary targets drawn from the same 24-byte target alphabet;\n- 32 initial target presentations per key;\n- two alternating learned renderings;\n- one never-trained third rendering for alias probes;\n- four A keys corrupted once after B;\n- four disjoint A keys revised eight times in rendering 0 after C;\n- eight untouched A keys that never return after A;\n- D as a final unrelated interference wave.\n\nAccounting must preserve the existing distinction between:\n\n- unique logical keys;\n- distinct trained renderings;\n- distinct raw 12-byte contexts;\n- context/target pairs;\n- total target presentations;\n- total byte presentations.\n\nNominal full-trajectory counts remain:\n\n- **64** unique logical keys;\n- **128** trained target renderings;\n- **64** held-out target renderings;\n- **2,084** target prediction events;\n- **29,176** total next-byte prediction events.\n\nRepeated presentations are never counted as new examples.\n\nSeeds, not byte events, are the statistical units.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Overall staged design\n\n## Stage I — guaranteed\n\nFour arms × eight fresh seeds:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{32\\text{ trajectories}}\n\\]\n\nScientific seeds:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{35101,\\ldots,35108}.\n\\]\n\nArms:\n\n1. `H-O`\n2. `H-P`\n3. `H-RLS`\n4. `DICT`\n\n## Stage II — conditional and already fixed\n\nRuns if **any** Stage-I shared-memory qualification condition fails.\n\nFour arms × eight different fresh seeds:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{32\\text{ trajectories}}\n\\]\n\nScientific seeds:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{35201,\\ldots,35208}.\n\\]\n\nArms:\n\n1. `BASE`\n2. `SHARED-R`\n3. `LOCAL-R`\n4. `LOCAL-V`\n\nThe Stage-II specification is locked before Stage-I scientific outcomes.\n\nNo parameter or arm in Stage II may change after Stage I.\n\nBefore execution, both seed blocks must be checked against all accessible prior manifests and embedded seed lists. If a collision is found before any scientific outcomes exist, replace the **entire affected block** with the next unused consecutive eight-seed block and lock the replacement.\n\nEngineering/smoke seeds must be disjoint.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Stage I — exact shared-solver isolate\n\n## 6.1 Scientific question\n\nThe remaining shared-memory confound is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\text{Does a good shared solution exist, while the greedy writer simply fails to track it?}\n}\n\\]\n\nV33 explicitly left this unresolved.\n\nV34 did not test it.\n\nStage I therefore returns to the simpler V33 lag96 / hard-bank substrate and changes only the writer.\n\nDo **not** use V34's KC expansion.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Stage-I common substrate\n\nUse the exact V33 lag96 representation:\n\n- last 12 raw bytes;\n- eight signed binary bits per byte;\n- chronological lag order;\n- zero-fill before 12 bytes exist;\n- one global L2 normalization.\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\nz_t\\in\\mathbb R^{96}.\n\\]\n\nUse the exact fixed eight-bank routing centers and argmax rule inherited from V32/V33.\n\nNo routing learning.\n\nNo bank allocation.\n\nNo encoder learning.\n\nNo representation reset.\n\n### Strict-retention routing clarification\n\nThe V33 same-key bank-agreement statistics for different aliases do **not** impose a ceiling on the primary strict-retention endpoint.\n\nUntouched-A retention is probed on the same two exact trained renderings that originally wrote the memory. Routing is deterministic for an exact context, so each strict probe returns to the same bank used by that rendering during acquisition.\n\nTherefore Stage I does **not** add a single-bank live arm.\n\nBank mismatch remains relevant for:\n\n- known-alias transfer;\n- held-out-alias transfer;\n- cross-rendering revision.\n\nThose endpoints must be reported both overall and conditioned on whether the probe routes to a bank that was written by either trained rendering of that logical key.\n\n---\n\n# 8. Stage-I arms\n\n| Arm | Representation / routing | Writer | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **H-O** | lag96 + fixed 8-bank router | ordinary 0.6 writer | fresh ordinary control |\n| **H-P** | identical | V33 protected full covariance | validated interference-reduction control |\n| **H-RLS** | identical | exact online ridge / RLS | shared-solver test |\n| **DICT** | exact raw last-12-byte context | existing cumulative-count dictionary | growing exact-storage reference |\n\nThe dictionary is retained for historical continuity. Its revision behavior is **not** used as evidence against item-local memory in general.\n\n---\n\n# 9. Exact H-RLS definition\n\nFor each bank \\(b\\), maintain:\n\n\\[\nW_b\\in\\mathbb R^{96\\times256},\n\\qquad\nP_b\\in\\mathbb R^{96\\times96}.\n\\]\n\nInitialize:\n\n\\[\nW_b=0,\n\\qquad\nP_b=I.\n\\]\n\nFor current normalized code \\(z\\), routed to bank \\(b\\), and observed one-hot next-byte vector \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\ng\n=\n\\frac{P_bz}{1+z^\\top P_bz},\n\\]\n\n\\[\nW_b^+\n=\nW_b\n+\ng\n\\left(y-W_b^\\top z\\right)^\\top,\n\\]\n\n\\[\nP_b^+\n=\nP_b\n-\n\\frac{P_bzz^\\top P_b}\n{1+z^\\top P_bz}.\n\\]\n\nThis corresponds to the exact cumulative ridge objective:\n\n\\[\nW_t\n=\n\\arg\\min_W\n\\left[\n\\sum_{\\tau\\le t}\n\\|y_\\tau-W^\\top z_\\tau\\|_2^2\n+\n\\|W\\|_F^2\n\\right]\n\\]\n\nwithin each routed bank.\n\nThere is:\n\n- no 0.6 correction factor in H-RLS;\n- no directional cap;\n- no forgetting factor;\n- no finite horizon;\n- no replay;\n- no phase-dependent weighting;\n- no special treatment of targets, revisions, or noise.\n\nReported predictive probabilities use the same historical temperature:\n\n\\[\nT=0.1.\n\\]\n\nThe RLS regression target is the one-hot **score vector**, consistent with the existing raw-score writers.\n\n### Why there is no forgetting-factor RLS arm\n\nV35 intentionally tests the exact all-history shared optimum first.\n\nExponentially weighted RLS would answer a different question:\n\n\\[\n\\text{where should the solver sit on a recency/stability frontier?}\n\\]\n\nThe program already has repeated evidence that finite horizons can trade retention against new learning. V35 therefore does not add an EW-RLS arm.\n\nIf exact H-RLS retains but fails revision while O2/O3 show that the current revised facts are jointly representable, classify the result as **catastrophic remembering / stale-history weighting** rather than as evidence that shared linear capacity is impossible.\n\n---\n\n# 10. Mandatory numerical verification for H-RLS\n\nScientific execution is blocked unless all deterministic RLS tests pass.\n\nFor several fixed random and adversarial sequences, compare online H-RLS against direct batch ridge:\n\n\\[\nW_{\\mathrm{RLS}}\n\\approx\n(Z^\\top Z+I)^{-1}Z^\\top Y.\n\\]\n\nTest:\n\n- random full-rank streams;\n- repeated identical \\(z\\);\n- orthogonal \\(z\\);\n- nearly collinear \\(z\\);\n- repeated labels;\n- contradictory labels on the same \\(z\\);\n- multiple output dimensions;\n- bank separation.\n\nRequire strict float64 agreement.\n\nAlso verify:\n\n- \\(P=P^\\top\\) numerically;\n- \\(P\\) remains positive definite;\n- direct solve and RLS scores agree;\n- pause/resume is bit-stable where expected;\n- evaluator probes do not modify \\(W\\) or \\(P\\).\n\n---\n\n# 11. Stage-I evaluator-only solvability oracles\n\nA negative H-RLS result is otherwise ambiguous.\n\nTherefore V35 adds **read-only, noncausal diagnostic fits**. They never produce live predictions and never change any learner.\n\nThese oracles are essential to distinguish optimization failure, stale-history weighting, and representational capacity.\n\n## 11.1 Oracle O0 — all-history direct ridge\n\nAt each major checkpoint, reconstruct the sufficient statistics for **all byte events** observed by H-RLS and solve the exact batch ridge problem directly.\n\nThis must match H-RLS.\n\nPurpose:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{implementation verification}}\n\\]\n\nnot a separate scientific arm.\n\nAny material discrepancy invalidates H-RLS.\n\n## 11.2 Oracle O1 — target-history ridge\n\nEvaluator-only, use only target-position events accumulated so far:\n\n\\[\n(Z_T,Y_T).\n\\]\n\nFit per routed bank:\n\n\\[\nW_T=(Z_T^\\top Z_T+I)^{-1}Z_T^\\top Y_T.\n\\]\n\nThis oracle uses evaluator knowledge of target positions and is therefore **not deployable**.\n\nPurpose:\n\n> Determine whether the thousands of ordinary syntax/non-target byte constraints are crowding the arbitrary target associations in the all-byte shared objective.\n\nInterpretation:\n\n- H-RLS poor, O1 strong → ordinary stream objective crowding is important.\n- H-RLS and O1 both poor → target facts themselves are mutually difficult under this shared linear substrate or stale target history.\n\n## 11.3 Oracle O2 — current-fact ridge\n\nAt each after-wave checkpoint construct **one evaluator copy of every currently valid trained target rendering**.\n\nFor each context, pair its lag96 code with the target that is currently intended:\n\n- original targets for ordinary untouched facts;\n- new targets for genuinely revised facts after revision;\n- one-off corrupt outcomes do **not** redefine the intended current fact.\n\nFit per bank:\n\n\\[\nW_{\\mathrm{current},1}\n=\n(Z_C^\\top Z_C+I)^{-1}Z_C^\\top Y_C.\n\\]\n\nThis removes stale-history weighting and repeated-presentation imbalance.\n\nPurpose:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Can the current facts coexist under the same lag96 + hard-bank linear substrate?}}\n\\]\n\n## 11.4 Oracle O3 — current-fact minimum-norm fit\n\nUsing the same current-fact set, compute the Moore–Penrose least-squares / minimum-norm solution with fixed numerical tolerance.\n\nNo tuning from outcomes.\n\nThis is a pure representability diagnostic.\n\nIf O3 cannot score the current trained target contexts highly, then no online solver can make this exact fixed linear substrate satisfy them all simultaneously.\n\nIf O3 is near-perfect while H-RLS fails, the problem is **objective/history/solver dynamics**, not static linear representability.\n\n---\n\n# 12. Stage-I primary endpoints\n\nPrimary:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{untouched A accuracy after D}}\n\\]\n\naveraged across the two exact trained renderings.\n\nAlways display:\n\n\\[\nA_{\\text{after A}}\n\\]\n\nbeside it.\n\nAlso report:\n\n- A after B;\n- A after C;\n- D-current accuracy;\n- whole-stream bits/byte;\n- target-only bits/byte;\n- non-target bits/byte;\n- first target occurrence;\n- second logical-key occurrence;\n- first exact-rendering repeat;\n- revision prequential accuracy at 1/2/4/8 changed outcomes;\n- exact / known-alias / held-out revision probes;\n- obsolete-old-target probability;\n- one-off corruption damage;\n- state bytes and operation-count proxy.\n\nOracle scores are reported separately and explicitly labeled noncausal.\n\nFor known-alias and held-out-alias probes, also report:\n\n- route match to either trained rendering's bank;\n- route miss;\n- accuracy and obsolete-target probability separately within those subsets.\n\nDo **not** route-condition the primary untouched-A exact-rendering endpoint; its exact contexts deterministically return to their training banks.\n\nBits/byte remains a required diagnostic but is not a Stage-I qualification gate.\n\n---\n\n# 13. Stage-I acquisition validity\n\nAn arm cannot establish persistence by learning nothing.\n\nFor H-O, H-P, and H-RLS separately require:\n\n1. mean untouched A after A \\(\\ge90\\%\\);\n2. minimum-seed untouched A after A \\(\\ge75\\%\\);\n3. mean D-current \\(\\ge90\\%\\).\n\nIf H-RLS fails acquisition validity, it cannot qualify shared memory.\n\n---\n\n# 14. Stage-I statistical protocol\n\nUse the eight seeds as independent units.\n\nAll main contrasts use complete paired two-sided 95% Student-\\(t\\) intervals.\n\nPrimary contrast:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{H\\!-\\!RLS}-\\mathrm{H\\!-\\!P}}.\n\\]\n\nNo byte-level pseudo-replication.\n\nAlways report absolute levels beside differences.\n\nSecondary intervals remain descriptive unless explicitly included in a gate.\n\n## C0 — second fresh-seed replication of the covariance primitive\n\nStage I also preregisters:\n\n\\[\n\x08oxed{\\mathrm{H\\!-\\!P}-\\mathrm{H\\!-\\!O}}\n\\]\n\non untouched-A after D as a continuity/replication endpoint.\n\nHistorical reference levels:\n\n| round | H-O A-after-D | H-P A-after-D | H-P − H-O |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| V33 | 5.47% | 21.88% | +16.41 pp |\n| V34 fresh seeds | 7.03% | 15.62% | +8.59 pp |\n| V35 | preregistered | preregistered | preregistered |\n\nV35 is therefore a **second fresh-seed replication**, not the first.\n\nReport:\n\n- H-O and H-P acquisition validity;\n- paired H-P − H-O A-after-D difference and 95% interval;\n- D-current;\n- target bpb;\n- all eight paired seed values.\n\nC0 does **not** determine whether Stage II runs. Its purpose is to estimate the reproducibility and magnitude stability of the validated covariance interference-reduction effect.\n\n---\n\n# 15. Stage-I shared-memory qualification\n\nStage II is skipped **only if every condition S1–S6 passes**.\n\nBits/byte is reported prominently but is **not** a Stage-I skip/run gate. H-RLS optimizes a squared-error ridge objective in raw score space, and its score scale can differ from H-P under the fixed historical softmax temperature. A storage mechanism should not be rejected solely because its uncalibrated score scale yields worse bpb.\n\nNo temperature may be fitted on V35 scientific outcomes.\n\nIf H-RLS passes S1–S6 but has poor fixed-temperature bpb, report:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\texttt{shared_storage_qualified__calibration_unresolved}}\n\\]\n\nrather than treating the storage result as a failure.\n\n## S1 — acquisition validity\n\nH-RLS passes Section 13.\n\n## S2 — absolute long-horizon persistence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\operatorname{mean}(A_{\\text{after D}}^{RLS})\\ge75\\%\n}\n\\]\n\non the untouched cohort.\n\n## S3 — material improvement over protected directional writing\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{mean}\n(A_{\\text{after D}}^{RLS}\n-\nA_{\\text{after D}}^{H-P})\n\\ge20\\text{ pp},\n\\]\n\nwith paired 95% lower bound \\(>0\\).\n\n## S4 — current-learning noninferiority\n\nPaired 95% lower bound for:\n\n\\[\nD_{\\text{current}}^{RLS}\n-\nD_{\\text{current}}^{H-P}\n\\]\n\nmust be:\n\n\\[\n\\ge-5\\text{ pp}.\n\\]\n\n## S5 — early-learning noncollapse\n\nAt the second logical-key occurrence on new B/C/D keys:\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{mean}\n(\\mathrm{RLS}-\\mathrm{H-P})\n\\ge-15\\text{ pp}.\n\\]\n\nAlso report first exact-rendering repeat separately.\n\n## S6 — revision plasticity\n\nA persistent learner must also forget obsolete facts when experience changes.\n\nFor the **exact revised rendering** require:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{revision accuracy after 4 changed outcomes}\\ge90\\%}\n\\]\n\nand:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{obsolete-target probability after 8 changed outcomes}\\le0.10}.\n\\]\n\nKnown-alias and held-out-alias revision remain reported but are **not Stage-I gates**, because V33/V34 established alias propagation as a distinct representation/addressing problem.\n\nFor those alias probes, additionally report performance conditional on route match versus route miss as defined in Section 7.\n\n### Stage-I bpb diagnostic\n\nStill report:\n\n- whole-stream bpb;\n- target-only bpb;\n- non-target bpb;\n- H-RLS − H-P paired differences and intervals.\n\nInterpret those values as predictive-calibration/utility diagnostics under the fixed historical temperature, not as the primary solver qualification.\n\n---\n\n# 16. Stage-I interpretation matrix\n\nThe evaluator oracles are used to classify failure, not to rescue a failed live arm.\n\n## Case I — H-RLS passes S1–S6\n\nConclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{shared parametric memory survives the exact-solver test}}\n\\]\n\nStage II does not run.\n\nThe next round should investigate:\n\n- efficient/local RLS approximations;\n- longer horizons;\n- sample efficiency;\n- natural byte data;\n- bounded state.\n\n## Case II — retention high, revision fails\n\nIf H-RLS retains old A strongly but fails S6 while O2/O3 fit the current revised facts well:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{catastrophic remembering from infinite stale history}}\n\\]\n\nis the leading diagnosis.\n\nDo **not** conclude shared linear capacity is impossible.\n\nStage II still runs because the user's target includes revision.\n\n## Case III — H-RLS poor, O1 strong\n\nThe all-byte shared objective is crowding target facts.\n\nThis supports architectural separation between:\n\n\\[\n\\text{shared stream statistics}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\text{fact-specific state}.\n\\]\n\nStage II runs.\n\n## Case IV — H-RLS poor, O1 poor, O2/O3 strong\n\nThe current facts are representable, but cumulative historical weighting is the problem.\n\nThis includes stale revisions and accumulated incompatible observations.\n\nStage II runs.\n\n## Case V — O3 itself poor\n\nThen the fixed lag96 + hard-bank **linear** substrate cannot simultaneously fit the current target facts well.\n\nThat is evidence for a representation/storage-capacity limitation independent of the online writer.\n\nStage II runs.\n\n---\n\n# 17. Why Stage II was simplified from V35 v1\n\nThe first V35 draft proposed a 256-slot episodic mechanism containing all of the following simultaneously:\n\n- approximate cosine retrieval;\n- top-4 slot mixture;\n- learned key centroids;\n- a surprise threshold;\n- support counters;\n- deterministic eviction;\n- bounded capacity;\n- residual fusion;\n- local feedback.\n\nThat design would have reintroduced the project's historical “mechanism soup” problem.\n\nA failure would not reveal whether the culprit was:\n\n- ownership;\n- addressing;\n- allocation;\n- alias pooling;\n- capacity;\n- eviction;\n- base drift;\n- or residual semantics.\n\nTherefore all approximate/bounded resource-management machinery is **deferred to V36**.\n\nV35 Stage II tests only local ownership.\n\n---\n\n# 18. A second flaw in the old Stage-II design: residual state can become stale\n\nSuppose a local memory stores an additive residual:\n\n\\[\ns=s_B+r_c.\n\\]\n\nEven if \\(r_c\\) is never touched by an unrelated example, the shared base score \\(s_B\\) can drift.\n\nThen the final prediction for context \\(c\\) can change despite the local memory being perfectly preserved.\n\nTherefore failure of an additive exact-context residual does **not** prove local ownership failed.\n\nV35 v2 explicitly separates:\n\n- **local residual ownership** (`LOCAL-R`);\n- **local absolute ownership** (`LOCAL-V`).\n\nThis is one of the principal refinements.\n\n---\n\n# 19. Stage II — Local Ownership Isolate\n\nStage II runs if **any** S1–S6 condition fails.\n\nScientific question:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\text{Does item-local state prevent long-horizon overwrite when addressing is exact?}\n}\n\\]\n\nIt is intentionally an **unbounded diagnostic isolate**.\n\nIt is not yet the final bounded architecture.\n\n---\n\n# 20. Stage-II shared base predictor\n\nAll four Stage-II arms use the exact historical V32 **current-code full-covariance** causal byte learner as their shared base.\n\nThis choice is deliberate.\n\nV33 showed that:\n\n- the lag/full system was substantially better on arbitrary target memory;\n- the V32 current/full system was substantially better on whole-stream prediction.\n\nThat is exactly the division of labor V35 wants to test.\n\nThe Stage-II base therefore uses the source-locked V32 implementation:\n\n\\[\n[\\text{last},F,F-S]\n\\]\n\nwith its original:\n\n- seeded receptor vectors;\n- fast/slow constants;\n- eight fixed banks;\n- full covariance writer;\n- 0.6 score correction;\n- direction cap;\n- temperature 0.1.\n\nThe implementation must be copied from the archived V32 source, not reconstructed from prose.\n\nPin the archived provenance in the manifest:\n\n- V32 source SHA-256: `aa3c903796b4d23fdcaccb2a80aa376af71999c5ef7a03fa2b7c95c3f3b6e626`\n- V33 source SHA-256 used as the lag96/address-lineage reference: `fe45c72d7bc34acafe7f36e714859bd292a447ab8c6af573b58246ec424df500`\n\nIf the archived files available to Codex do not hash to these values, scientific execution must stop rather than silently substitute a reconstruction.\n\nBefore science, an engineering trajectory must reproduce archived V32 current/full metrics and final learned-state hash where a reference hash is available.\n\n---\n\n# 21. Base independence across Stage-II arms\n\nThis requirement is absolute.\n\nLet the base raw score be:\n\n\\[\ns_B.\n\\]\n\nThe base learner always updates **exactly as if no auxiliary memory existed**:\n\n\\[\ne_B=y-s_B\n\\]\n\nin the original V32 raw-score sense.\n\nAuxiliary memory output never changes:\n\n- base routing;\n- base covariance;\n- base update direction;\n- base residual;\n- base state.\n\nFor a given seed, the final base-state hash must be bit-identical across:\n\n- BASE;\n- SHARED-R;\n- LOCAL-R;\n- LOCAL-V.\n\nIf not, Stage II is invalid.\n\nThis preserves the fly-inspired interpretation:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{private memory beside a shared predictor, not hidden co-training of the base}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n# 22. Stage-II private address channel\n\nIndependently compute the exact V33 lag96 code:\n\n\\[\nk_t\\in\\mathbb R^{96}.\n\\]\n\nAlso retain the exact causal raw address:\n\n\\[\nc_t=\\text{last 12 raw bytes}.\n\\]\n\nUses:\n\n- `SHARED-R` uses \\(k_t\\) as its shared residual feature.\n- `LOCAL-R` and `LOCAL-V` use exact raw \\(c_t\\) as the item-local address.\n\nNo logical-key or alias metadata enters addressing.\n\nTwo syntactic renderings are therefore two different exact addresses unless the model later acquires an explicit alias mechanism. V35 does not add such a mechanism.\n\n---\n\n# 23. Common error-triggered eligibility schedule\n\nV35 Stage II needs a causal way to decide when local exception state exists, without smuggling in a task-position flag.\n\nThe rule is parameter-free.\n\nAt prediction time compute the **base-only** top-1 prediction:\n\n\\[\n\\hat y_B=\\arg\\max_j s_{B,j}.\n\\]\n\nAfter the true byte \\(y\\) arrives:\n\n- if \\(\\hat y_B\\ne y\\), mark exact context \\(c_t\\) **eligible forever**;\n- otherwise do not newly activate it.\n\nA newly eligible context may receive its first auxiliary-memory update immediately after the outcome that activated it.\n\nOnce eligible, it remains eligible.\n\nCritically:\n\n- the schedule depends only on the source-locked base and observed byte;\n- it is identical for all four arms;\n- it is **not** interpreted as a revision detector;\n- V35 makes no claim that error-triggered allocation is biologically special.\n\nThis avoids repeating the V26/V31 mistake of praising a gate without matched controls.\n\n`BASE` computes and records the same eligibility schedule evaluator-side but has no auxiliary memory.\n\nFor every seed, the eligibility-set hash must match exactly across all four arms.\n\n---\n\n# 24. Eligibility-coverage safeguard\n\nBecause eligibility is not the mechanism under test, a negative ownership result is interpretable only if the relevant facts actually became eligible.\n\nReport, by first/second/fourth/eighth/terminal acquisition:\n\n- fraction of trained target renderings eligible;\n- fraction of untouched-A trained renderings eligible;\n- fraction of all distinct exact contexts eligible;\n- total eligible-context count.\n\nIf fewer than:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{90\\%}\n\\]\n\nof untouched-A trained renderings are eligible by the end of A acquisition, then a failure of LOCAL-R/LOCAL-V on strict A retention is classified as:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{eligibility inadequate; ownership not falsified}}\n\\]\n\nrather than evidence against item-local state.\n\nA positive result remains valid even if coverage is lower, provided absolute retention is reported honestly.\n\n---\n\n# 25. Stage-II arm BASE\n\nPrediction:\n\n\\[\ns=s_B.\n\\]\n\nNo auxiliary memory.\n\nThe base still computes the common eligibility schedule for matching and diagnostics.\n\nPurpose:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{shared statistical predictor reference}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n# 26. Stage-II arm SHARED-R\n\nPurpose:\n\n> Test whether adding a separate residual pathway helps even when that residual state is still globally shared.\n\nMaintain one matrix:\n\n\\[\nR\\in\\mathbb R^{96\\times256},\n\\qquad\nR_0=0.\n\\]\n\nIf exact context \\(c_t\\) was already eligible before prediction:\n\n\\[\ns=s_B+R^\\top k_t.\n\\]\n\nOtherwise:\n\n\\[\ns=s_B.\n\\]\n\nAfter observing \\(y\\):\n\n1. update the base independently using \\(s_B\\);\n2. apply the common eligibility rule;\n3. if \\(c_t\\) is eligible after this event, update \\(R\\) from the raw score actually used for the auxiliary prediction:\n\n\\[\ne=y-s_{\\mathrm{used}},\n\\]\n\n\\[\nq_0=\\frac{k_t}{k_t^\\top k_t},\n\\]\n\n\\[\nR^+\n=\nR+0.6\\,q_0e^\\top.\n\\]\n\nNo covariance, no cap, no separate router.\n\nThis arm still has shared residual coefficients and can therefore suffer interference.\n\n---\n\n# 27. Stage-II arm LOCAL-R\n\nPurpose:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{isolate item-local coefficients while retaining additive residual semantics}}\n\\]\n\nFor each eligible exact context \\(c\\), store:\n\n\\[\nr_c\\in\\mathbb R^{256}.\n\\]\n\nInitial:\n\n\\[\nr_c=0.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(c_t\\) was already eligible before prediction:\n\n\\[\ns=s_B+r_{c_t}.\n\\]\n\nOtherwise:\n\n\\[\ns=s_B.\n\\]\n\nAfter outcome:\n\n1. update base independently;\n2. apply common eligibility;\n3. if context is eligible, update only its local vector:\n\n\\[\nr_c^+\n=\nr_c+\n0.6\\left(y-s_{\\mathrm{used}}\\right).\n\\]\n\nNo other context's local vector changes.\n\nThis gives exact coefficient ownership but remains vulnerable to later drift of the shared base \\(s_B\\).\n\nThat vulnerability is intentional and measured.\n\n---\n\n# 28. Stage-II arm LOCAL-V\n\nPurpose:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{test absolute item-local ownership, immune to later base drift}}\n\\]\n\nFor each eligible exact context \\(c\\), store an absolute local score vector:\n\n\\[\nv_c\\in\\mathbb R^{256}.\n\\]\n\nInitial:\n\n\\[\nv_c=0.\n\\]\n\nIf context \\(c_t\\) was already eligible before prediction, its local predictor **overrides** the shared base:\n\n\\[\ns=v_{c_t}.\n\\]\n\nIf it was not yet eligible:\n\n\\[\ns=s_B.\n\\]\n\nAfter the observed byte:\n\n1. update the base independently from \\(s_B\\);\n2. apply the common eligibility rule;\n3. if \\(c_t\\) is eligible, update only:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nv_c^+\n=\n0.4v_c+0.6y\n}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(y\\) denotes the one-hot score vector.\n\nEquivalently:\n\n\\[\nv_c^+=v_c+0.6(y-v_c).\n\\]\n\nThis reuses the historical 0.6 immediate-correction scale without tuning.\n\nUnrelated contexts cannot alter \\(v_c\\).\n\nLater drift of \\(s_B\\) cannot alter the prediction whenever \\(c\\) retrieves its local value.\n\nThis is the cleanest ownership isolate in V35.\n\n---\n\n# 29. Stage-II prediction and loss convention\n\nAll reported probabilities use:\n\n\\[\np=\\operatorname{softmax}(s/0.1).\n\\]\n\nAll auxiliary updates use **raw score errors**, consistent with V32/V33/V34.\n\nDo not replace the historical score update with probability-gradient residuals.\n\nFor newly activated contexts, the prediction for the activation event was still the base prediction; the auxiliary state learns only after the byte is observed.\n\nThis preserves causality.\n\n---\n\n# 30. Why Stage II does not use approximate alias retrieval\n\nV33 and V34 already demonstrated that:\n\n- exact persistence;\n- alias generalization;\n- revision transfer across aliases;\n\nare different capabilities.\n\nV35 Stage II isolates exact ownership.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- no cosine threshold;\n- no top-k slot retrieval;\n- no key-centroid learning;\n- no alias pooling;\n- no learned identity;\n- no semantic key.\n\nKnown-alias and held-out-alias probes remain visible diagnostics but **do not gate ownership qualification**.\n\nIf LOCAL-V preserves exact trained contexts but does not revise another alias, that is not a failure of the ownership hypothesis.\n\nIt means addressing/invariance remains the next problem.\n\n---\n\n# 31. Why Stage II is intentionally unbounded\n\nThe first V35 draft mixed ownership with a 256-slot capacity limit and eviction policy.\n\nThat was premature.\n\n`LOCAL-R` and `LOCAL-V` therefore store every eligible exact context for this diagnostic workload.\n\nState growth must be reported honestly:\n\n- number of eligible contexts;\n- key bytes;\n- local vector bytes;\n- Python/object overhead estimate;\n- total resident state.\n\nA success does **not** qualify a bounded lifelong learner.\n\nIt establishes only:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{item-local ownership is a useful missing primitive}}\n\\]\n\nand licenses a later bounded-capacity experiment.\n\nBounded slots, eviction, approximate addressing, and consolidation belong to V36 if warranted.\n\n---\n\n# 32. Stage-II primary causal contrasts\n\n## R1 — separate shared residual pathway\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{SHARED-R}-\\text{BASE}}\n\\]\n\nThis asks whether adding a residual-exception pathway under the fixed common eligibility schedule helps.\n\nDo not attribute any effect specifically to error-triggered eligibility; the schedule is not independently tested.\n\n## R2 — item-local coefficient ownership\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{LOCAL-R}-\\text{SHARED-R}}\n\\]\n\nThis is the principal ownership contrast.\n\nThe eligibility schedule and base are identical.\n\nThe critical difference is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{shared residual coefficients}\n\\quad\\text{vs}\\quad\n\\text{exact-context local residual coefficients}.\n\\]\n\n## R3 — absolute ownership versus additive residual ownership\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{LOCAL-V}-\\text{LOCAL-R}}\n\\]\n\nThis tests whether a preserved local residual is still defeated by drift in the shared base.\n\nA large R3 effect would establish:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{local state must own the prediction itself, not merely an offset to a drifting base}}\n\\]\n\non this workload.\n\n---\n\n# 33. Stage-II acquisition validity\n\nFor each live arm report:\n\n- A after A;\n- D-current;\n- first target occurrence;\n- second logical occurrence;\n- first exact-rendering repeat.\n\nA candidate cannot establish persistence through refusal to learn.\n\nFor ownership qualification, LOCAL-V must satisfy:\n\n1. mean A after A \\(\\ge90\\%\\);\n2. minimum seed A after A \\(\\ge75\\%\\);\n3. mean D-current \\(\\ge90\\%\\).\n\nBecause exact aliases have distinct local addresses, second logical-key occurrence is not an exact-repeat measure. The first exact-rendering repeat is the more relevant local-memory sample-efficiency diagnostic.\n\n---\n\n# 34. Stage-II ownership qualification\n\nA strong LOCAL-V ownership result requires all L1–L6 below.\n\n## L1 — eligibility coverage\n\nAt least 90% of untouched-A trained renderings eligible by end of A acquisition.\n\nIf this fails, a negative ownership result is inconclusive.\n\n## L2 — acquisition validity\n\nLOCAL-V passes Section 33.\n\n## L3 — absolute persistence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\operatorname{mean}(A_{\\text{after D}}^{LOCAL-V})\\ge90\\%\n}\n\\]\n\non untouched A.\n\nThe threshold is deliberately higher than the historical 75% “promising” level because exact item-local ownership should nearly eliminate unrelated overwrite if the hypothesis is correct.\n\nAlso report the historical 75% threshold for continuity.\n\n## L4 — ownership advantage\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{mean}\n(A_{\\text{after D}}^{LOCAL-V}\n-\nA_{\\text{after D}}^{SHARED-R})\n\\ge20\\text{ pp}\n\\]\n\nwith paired 95% lower bound \\(>0\\).\n\nAlso report LOCAL-R−SHARED-R and LOCAL-V−LOCAL-R.\n\n## L5 — fast exact revision\n\nFor rendering 0 of genuinely revised A:\n\n- mean prequential accuracy on the **second changed occurrence** \\(\\ge75\\%\\);\n- exact revised-rendering probe after four changed outcomes \\(\\ge90\\%\\);\n- obsolete-old-target probability after eight changed outcomes \\(\\le0.10\\).\n\nThis prevents “perfect memory” from becoming catastrophic remembering.\n\nKnown and held-out aliases are reported but not gated.\n\n## L6 — whole-stream noninferiority\n\nMean:\n\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{LOCAL-V}}\n-\nH_{\\mathrm{BASE}}\n\\le0.10\\text{ bits/byte}.\n\\]\n\nReport paired 95% interval.\n\nAn auxiliary memory that preserves facts while seriously degrading ordinary byte prediction is not a satisfactory two-system candidate.\n\n---\n\n# 35. Stage-II sample-efficiency reporting\n\nThe user’s primary research criterion remains sample efficiency.\n\nFor all Stage-II arms report:\n\n- first target occurrence;\n- second logical-key occurrence;\n- first exact-rendering repeat;\n- 4th, 8th, 16th, 32nd target presentation where applicable;\n- number of base errors required before eligibility;\n- fraction of target contexts eligible after their first occurrence;\n- fraction eligible after second occurrence.\n\nFor LOCAL-V, specifically report accuracy on the **first exact recurrence after local state has been created**.\n\nDo not describe terminal 32-presentation acquisition as one-shot learning.\n\n---\n\n# 36. Noise is diagnostic, not a V35 gate\n\nA one-off corruption and a genuine revision are observationally similar at the instant of contradiction.\n\nFast local revision can therefore increase vulnerability to one-off local noise.\n\nV35 does not introduce a noise/revision classifier.\n\nReport for the one-off corruption cohort:\n\n- pre-corruption exact accuracy;\n- immediate post-corruption accuracy;\n- old-target probability;\n- wrong-target probability;\n- whether the exact context was already eligible;\n- local-state norm/change.\n\nCrucially also report unrelated-A damage.\n\nFor LOCAL-V, unrelated local contexts should remain bit-for-bit unchanged.\n\nDo **not** fail ownership qualification merely because one erroneous outcome damages the exact corrupted item. That is the separate future problem of distinguishing transient noise from persistent change.\n\n---\n\n# 37. Stage-II immutability / locality tests\n\nFor LOCAL-R and LOCAL-V:\n\nOn every update, verify that:\n\n- only the exact current context's local vector can change;\n- all other local vectors remain bit-for-bit identical.\n\nFor SHARED-R:\n\n- the shared matrix may change globally.\n\nFor every Stage-II arm:\n\n- base state is bit-identical across arms for the same seed;\n- eligibility-set hash is bit-identical across arms for the same seed.\n\nThese are causal invariants, not optional diagnostics.\n\n---\n\n# 38. Stage-II read-only diagnostics\n\nReport:\n\n- number of eligible exact contexts by wave;\n- target versus non-target eligible counts;\n- target eligibility coverage curve;\n- local memory state bytes;\n- number of local vectors touched per event;\n- base-only accuracy/loss;\n- final accuracy/loss;\n- norm of SHARED-R correction;\n- norm of LOCAL-R correction;\n- norm/confidence shape of LOCAL-V value;\n- base drift on untouched-A contexts between A and D;\n- LOCAL-R final prediction drift decomposed into:\n  - unchanged local residual;\n  - changed base score;\n- LOCAL-V independence from that base drift.\n\nThis decomposition is essential for R3 interpretation.\n\n---\n\n# 39. Stage-II interpretation tree\n\n## Case A — SHARED-R already solves persistence\n\nIf SHARED-R reaches high long-horizon retention while BASE does not:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{early statistical/residual separation was the missing architecture}}\n\\]\n\nItem-local state may be unnecessary at this scale.\n\nDo not complicate further before confirmation.\n\n## Case B — LOCAL-R strongly beats SHARED-R and remains high\n\nThen:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{item-local coefficient ownership matters}}\n\\]\n\nand additive residual semantics are sufficient on this workload.\n\nV36 should tackle bounded addressing/capacity.\n\n## Case C — LOCAL-R fails but LOCAL-V succeeds\n\nThen:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{item-local state must be anchored absolutely; additive residuals become stale as the base drifts}}\n\\]\n\nThis is a particularly informative outcome.\n\n## Case D — LOCAL-V retains exact contexts but cannot revise exact context\n\nThen local ownership is too rigid or the update is wrong.\n\nAudit before adding any global gate.\n\n## Case E — LOCAL-V succeeds exact persistence/revision but aliases fail\n\nExpected interpretation:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{storage ownership solved locally; alias/addressing remains separate}}\n\\]\n\nV36 should test approximate/bounded addressing one mechanism at a time.\n\n## Case F — LOCAL-V fails and eligibility coverage is high\n\nThen exact local ownership under this score update is not sufficient; audit the exact-context implementation and whether the probe really retrieves the same causal context.\n\nDo not jump directly to another fly mechanism.\n\n## Case G — LOCAL-V fails but eligibility coverage is low\n\nVerdict:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{ownership untested because causal allocation failed}}\n\\]\n\nThe next experiment should isolate allocation, not storage.\n\n---\n\n# 40. What V35 deliberately does not test\n\nV35 does **not** test:\n\n- new KC widths;\n- new sparsity fractions;\n- APL-like homeostasis;\n- new compartment counts;\n- covariance masking;\n- MESU;\n- finite-horizon RLS;\n- forgetting-factor / exponentially weighted RLS;\n- global revision detectors;\n- approximate cosine slots;\n- top-k episodic retrieval;\n- learned key centroids;\n- semantic identities;\n- 256-slot capacity limits;\n- eviction;\n- consolidation;\n- replay;\n- natural-language scale;\n- Transformer attention.\n\nThese are excluded to preserve causal interpretability.\n\n---\n\n# 41. Resource accounting\n\nFor every arm report separately:\n\n- fixed encoder bytes;\n- shared output weights;\n- covariance / inverse-statistic bytes;\n- RLS state;\n- shared residual state;\n- exact-context key bytes;\n- local residual/value bytes;\n- counters and routing state;\n- resident NumPy bytes;\n- approximate Python-object overhead;\n- peak temporary state if practical.\n\nStage II is explicitly unbounded with stream length.\n\nDo not call it a final lifelong-memory architecture.\n\n---\n\n# 42. Observer-effect and reproducibility requirements\n\nThe runner must include deterministic observer tests.\n\nFor at least one full engineering trajectory of each model family:\n\n1. run with evaluator diagnostics enabled;\n2. run with them disabled;\n3. require identical final learner state;\n4. require identical prequential losses.\n\nFor Stage II additionally require:\n\n- identical base hashes across all four arms;\n- identical eligibility-set hashes across all four arms.\n\nExecution standard:\n\n- one self-contained Python file;\n- one command;\n- NumPy/SciPy only unless strictly necessary;\n- one BLAS thread;\n- automatic macOS `caffeinate`;\n- process lock;\n- atomic per-job result commits;\n- deterministic resume;\n- source/protocol/plan hashes;\n- corruption and foreign-job rejection;\n- compact result archive;\n- no giant per-byte logit dumps or model snapshots.\n\nSuggested command:\n\n```bash\npython3 elm_v35_shared_solver_ownership.py --install-deps\n```\n\nThe same command resumes an identical run.\n\nStage II starts automatically only when the locked S1–S6 evaluation is false.\n\n---\n\n# 43. Statistical reporting\n\nEight scientific seeds per stage are the independent units.\n\nUse complete paired two-sided 95% Student-\\(t\\) intervals.\n\nDo not use byte events as independent replicates.\n\nAlways show:\n\n- raw accuracy;\n- raw bits/byte;\n- sample size;\n- per-seed values;\n- paired differences.\n\nNormalized retention fractions may be reported only beside absolute acquisition and retention levels, using the same historical denominator guards.\n\nNo post-hoc multiple-arm winner selection.\n\nThe design has prespecified causal contrasts rather than a tournament.\n\n## 43.1 Graded verdict taxonomy\n\nEvery prespecified causal contrast must receive both a **statistical-effect verdict** and, separately, an **architectural-qualification verdict**.\n\nUse the following fixed vocabulary.\n\n### `qualified`\n\nAll preregistered architectural gates for the relevant mechanism pass.\n\n### `supported_below_qualification`\n\nThe primary paired causal contrast is directionally established:\n\n\\[\n95\\%\\ \\text{lower bound}>0\n\\]\n\nbut one or more absolute or minimum-effect qualification thresholds are missed.\n\nExample: H-RLS improves A-after-D by +15 pp with CI entirely above zero but misses the +20 pp S3 requirement or the 75% S2 absolute threshold.\n\n### `favorable_unresolved_precision`\n\nThe point estimate favors the candidate, but the 95% interval includes zero.\n\n### `no_mean_advantage`\n\nThe paired mean effect is \\(\\le0\\) and the interval does not establish a positive effect.\n\n### `adverse`\n\nThe 95% upper bound is \\(<0\\), establishing a negative effect relative to the comparator.\n\n### `invalid_acquisition`\n\nThe relevant acquisition-validity gate fails, so retention cannot be interpreted as persistence.\n\n### `ownership_unresolved_due_to_eligibility`\n\nStage-II LOCAL-R/LOCAL-V fails while the L1 eligibility-coverage safeguard is not met.\n\nThese categories must appear in `SUMMARY.md`, not only in machine-readable detail.\n\nA mechanism can therefore be scientifically supported without being promoted to the architectural mainline.\n\n---\n\n# 44. Required result package\n\nThe final V35 archive should contain:\n\n- executing `.py`;\n- locked V35 v2 protocol;\n- `RUN_MANIFEST.json`;\n- `PLAN.json`;\n- `REPORT.json`;\n- `SUMMARY.md`;\n- `SUMMARY.csv`;\n- compact per-seed records;\n- acquisition curves;\n- A-retention-by-wave table;\n- revision curves;\n- obsolete-target probabilities;\n- noise diagnostics;\n- Stage-I RLS direct-solve verification;\n- Stage-I O0–O3 oracle results;\n- C0 V33/V34/V35 covariance replication table;\n- graded verdict taxonomy output for every preregistered causal contrast;\n- Stage-II eligibility coverage if executed;\n- Stage-II base-hash / eligibility-hash invariants;\n- Stage-II base-drift decomposition if executed;\n- state/resource accounting;\n- observer-effect audit;\n- artifact checksums.\n\nNo large per-byte output dump is required.\n\n---\n\n# 45. Claim hierarchy\n\nV35 must distinguish the following claims.\n\n## Claim A — covariance remains an interference primitive\n\nHistorical context only; H-P is the continuity control.\n\n## Claim B — exact cumulative shared solving matters\n\nSupported if H-RLS materially exceeds H-P in retention while remaining plastic.\n\n## Claim C — the fixed shared linear substrate can represent the current facts\n\nSupported by high O2/O3 current-fact oracle performance.\n\nThis is an evaluator-only representability claim, not a live-learning result.\n\n## Claim D — shared parametric memory is sufficient on this workload\n\nSupported only if H-RLS passes **all S1–S6**.\n\n## Claim E — item-local coefficient ownership matters\n\nSupported if LOCAL-R materially exceeds SHARED-R at matched base and eligibility.\n\n## Claim F — absolute item-local ownership matters\n\nSupported if LOCAL-V materially exceeds LOCAL-R and passes L1–L6.\n\n## Claim G — a bounded lifelong architecture has been found\n\n**V35 cannot support this claim.**\n\nBounded capacity and approximate addressing are deliberately deferred.\n\n---\n\n# 46. Expected scientific value of each possible result\n\nV35 is useful even if every candidate fails.\n\n### If H-RLS retains and revises\n\nWe discover that previous “memory substrate” failures were largely solver failures.\n\n### If H-RLS retains but cannot revise\n\nWe isolate catastrophic remembering from infinite historical weighting.\n\n### If H-RLS fails while O3 is excellent\n\nWe know the facts can coexist statically, but the cumulative objective/history dynamics are wrong.\n\n### If O3 itself fails\n\nWe have direct evidence that this fixed shared linear substrate lacks simultaneous representational capacity for the current fact set.\n\n### If LOCAL-R succeeds\n\nShared base drift was tolerable; local coefficient ownership is the missing primitive.\n\n### If only LOCAL-V succeeds\n\nWe learn something sharper:\n\n> a persistent fact needs a prediction state whose meaning does not depend on a continually drifting shared predictor.\n\n### If even exact LOCAL-V fails\n\nThe result points to an implementation/context-retrieval problem, not another generic anti-forgetting trick.\n\n---\n\n# 47. Central V35 falsifiable fork\n\nThe refined round is centered on:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{c}\n\\textbf{Can a correct cumulative shared solver jointly preserve and revise the facts?}\\\\[2mm]\n\\textbf{If not, does exact item-local ownership prevent unrelated overwrite?}\n\\end{array}\n}\n\\]\n\nThis is intentionally narrower than the first V35 draft.\n\nIf local ownership succeeds, the next round should ask:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\text{How can that ownership be made bounded, approximate, alias-aware, and resource-managed?}\n}\n\\]\n\n—not add another shared-weight protection rule.\n\n---\n\n# 48. Evidence basis for this refinement\n\nThe design is grounded in the supplied project artifacts:\n\n- `v32_results_and_verification.md`\n- `v33_results_and_verification.md`\n- `v34_scientific_results_audit.md`\n- `v34_protocol.md`\n- the BANC-derived learning-core and architecture-distillation reports\n- the older Liquid Baby / ELM cumulative-readout results\n\nKey prior empirical facts motivating the changes include:\n\n1. V33 full covariance gave a real retention gain over ordinary writing but only about 22% absolute A retention after D.\n2. V33 improved alias geometry and revision through the lag representation, while whole-stream prediction favored the older temporal current-code representation.\n3. V34 reduced covariance cap pathology and increased effective rank dramatically without preserving old A.\n4. V34 sparse/compartment mechanisms looked strong after one interference wave and collapsed after the next.\n5. Exact context storage retained old exact facts, while its count semantics—not explicit storage in general—caused slow revision.\n6. The earlier Liquid Baby lineage repeatedly favored cumulative sufficient-statistic readouts, motivating the exact RLS isolate.\n\nThese are treated as empirical constraints on V35, not as proofs of the eventual architecture.\n\n---\n\n# 49. Codex implementation notes added in v2\n\nThis Codex-ready revision makes the following final protocol decisions explicit:\n\n1. **No single-bank live RLS arm.** V33 alias bank-agreement values do not cap exact untouched-A retention because exact renderings deterministically return to their training banks.\n2. **No route-free global oracle is added.** A naive one-global-matrix fit changes the parameterization and would not cleanly isolate routing relative to eight independent bank matrices. Route-conditioned diagnostics are used only for alias/revision endpoints where routing mismatch is actually relevant.\n3. **No forgetting-factor RLS arm.** Exact all-history RLS is the intended mathematical isolate; stale-history rigidity is diagnosed by revision gates plus O2/O3.\n4. **Stage-I bpb is diagnostic, not a skip/run gate.** The fixed historical temperature is retained for comparability, but no scientific-data calibration is fitted.\n5. **C0 explicitly tracks covariance replication across V33, V34, and V35.**\n6. **Graded verdicts are mandatory.** Missing an architectural threshold does not erase a statistically supported sub-threshold mechanism effect.\n7. **Stage-II approximate/bounded episodic machinery remains deferred.** V35 tests exact ownership first.\n"
